editorial:
Store the original positions before sorting. For the array [8, 2, 8], the sorted pairs are (2,2), (8,1), (8,3). The answer for 8 is the position stored in the last pair: 3.

Sort pairs lexicographically. All pairs with value x become consecutive, ordered by increasing original position. Since no position exceeds n, upper_bound on (x,n) skips the entire group. If the predecessor exists and has value x, its position is the answer. Otherwise x is absent.
~~~
sort(a.begin(), a.end());  // a contains (value, 1-based position)
for (int x : queries) {
    auto it = upper_bound(a.begin(), a.end(), pair{x, n});
    if (it == a.begin() || prev(it)->first != x)
        cout << -1 << '\n';
    else
        cout << prev(it)->second << '\n';
}
~~~
Sorting costs O(n log n). The t lookups cost O(t log n) altogether. The pair array uses O(n) space; queries can be processed as they arrive. A map also works: overwrite last[value] on each visit, then use find for queries.
